From 7b19a3cec94f25009770540fc65627c17c0b6085 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Oberdorf <109311040+flipfloptech@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 13:00:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] io_uring/memmap: finish a short bulk allocation with
 reclaiming pages

io_region_allocate_pages() backs a kernel-allocated region with one
compound allocation and, when that fails, with alloc_pages_bulk_node(),
treating any short count as -ENOMEM.

The bulk allocator is not a reclaiming allocator.  It batches order-0
pages from the per-cpu lists of a zone that sits above its low
watermark plus the request, and when no zone qualifies it falls back to
the regular allocator for a single page; with memcg kmem accounting
online - the default as soon as any memory cgroup exists - a
__GFP_ACCOUNT request skips the batch and returns that one page.  So a
region whose compound allocation fails, which for a costly order (a
1 MiB region is order 8 with 4 KiB pages) is routine once the page
cache has filled and fragmented memory, comes back short, and the whole
registration fails with -ENOMEM while gigabytes of clean page cache
could be reclaimed.

Observed with a FUSE-over-io-uring server registering kernel-allocated
buffer rings of 32 x 1 MiB per queue: on a 62 GiB x86_64 host whose
54 GiB page cache had been churned by buffered 4 KiB random reads
(MemFree 345-472 MiB while registering, a few MiB of it in free
order >= 8 blocks of ZONE_NORMAL) the registration failed with -ENOMEM
in 10 of 10 attempts, each after five retries over 1.2 s.  With this
change, 0 of 10 failed under the same pressure (0 MiB free at
order >= 8, so every compound attempt failed and the page-by-page
finish carried the registration).

Finish a short region page by page with alloc_page() and the same gfp,
which may enter direct reclaim, as vm_area_alloc_pages() does when the
bulk allocator comes back short for vmalloc.  Fail with -ENOMEM only if
a single page cannot be had, or once the task has a fatal signal
pending (no reclaiming on behalf of a task that is going away).  The
failure path is unchanged and releases every page allocated so far.

Signed-off-by: Justin Oberdorf <109311040+flipfloptech@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 io_uring/memmap.c | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/io_uring/memmap.c b/io_uring/memmap.c
index d4d716946971..4727272ec52a 100644
--- a/io_uring/memmap.c
+++ b/io_uring/memmap.c
@@ -192,6 +192,22 @@ static int io_region_allocate_pages(struct io_mapped_region *mr,
 
 	nr_allocated = alloc_pages_bulk_node(gfp, NUMA_NO_NODE,
 					     mr->nr_pages, pages);
+	/*
+	 * The bulk allocator draws on the free lists above the low watermark
+	 * and reclaims for at most one page (it returns a single page under
+	 * memcg kmem accounting): finish a short region with the reclaiming
+	 * single-page allocator, as vm_area_alloc_pages() does.
+	 */
+	while (nr_allocated < mr->nr_pages) {
+		struct page *page;
+
+		if (fatal_signal_pending(current))
+			break;
+		page = alloc_page(gfp);
+		if (!page)
+			break;
+		pages[nr_allocated++] = page;
+	}
 	if (nr_allocated != mr->nr_pages) {
 		if (nr_allocated)
 			release_pages(pages, nr_allocated);
